# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files Git should IGNORE (never upload to GitHub).
#
# Why: raw research data can be large (hundreds of MB to GB). GitHub is for
# CODE and DOCUMENTATION, not for storing datasets. The dataset already has a
# permanent public home (its Dryad DOI), so re-uploading it here would be
# redundant AND would make this repository painfully slow to clone.
#
# Reproducibility is preserved because scripts/01_download_dataset.py can
# re-fetch the exact same data from Dryad on any machine.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Raw downloaded data — never committed, never edited.
data/raw/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep
!data/raw/_FOLDER_NOTES.md
# The manifest is small, and it is the provenance record proving WHICH version
# of the dataset was analysed and that it arrived intact. Keep it in Git even
# though the data themselves are excluded.
!data/raw/download_manifest.json

# Processed/derived data — regenerable from raw data by our scripts.
# Processed outputs ARE tracked. They are derived from the raw data, but they
# are also what all 82 checks in 31_verify_claims.py read -- excluding them made
# the verifier unrunnable on a fresh clone, which defeats the point of having it.
# ~17 MB, largest file 3.7 MB. The raw data (789 MB) stays out.
data/processed/*.npy

# Generated figures are regenerable; keep the repo light.
# Figures ARE tracked. They are deliverables, referenced by the reports and the
# README, and excluding them left broken image links in the GitHub view. ~2.2 MB.

# Python housekeeping
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.venv/
venv/

# OS clutter
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
